{"event": "llm", "round": 7, "seconds": 63.0, "tokens_in": 7444, "tokens_out": 395, "content": "Design (doublets, depth, layout, levelised cost, CO2): 2 x 3000 m, boiler+electric, 79.3 EUR/MWh, 6.2 kt CO2  \nWhy: Below a certain return temperature the cost stops falling, because the 40 C brine cooling limit, not the return temperature, caps the heat taken from the brine. best_return_c is the highest return with the lowest cost, which keeps the most margin for the network.  \nReturn temperature: 40.0 C  \nUnplanned failure: backup_peak_mw_with_failure is 15.45 MW, levelised cost with backup for failure is 81.2 EUR/MWh  \nUncertainty (P10-P90 cost, how often the rule is met, how often it beats the alternative): P10-P90 cost is 77.14-87.23 EUR/MWh, meets geothermal-first rule 97.5% of the time, and beats the alternative (boiler+absorption) 65% of the time in cost and 100% in CO2.  \nGEMINI check: All 365 days ran successfully, no days failing checks, worst brine cooling is 39.95 C, flow and heat differences are within 0.48% and 0.19% respectively.  \nAssumptions: The design assumes a heating return temperature of 40 C, which is the lowest cost option while respecting the brine cooling limit. The layout uses boiler+electric, which is efficient and meets the geothermal-first rule. The depth of 3000 m provides sufficient brine temperature for efficient heating.  \n\nRECOMMENDATION: doublets=2, depth_m=3000, layout=boiler+electric", "tool_calls": null}
